+The decisive finding is not the cost verdict but *what the signal trades*.
+`research/backtest/regime_stability.py::momentum_signals` computes
+`mean(log(closes[i-lookback+1:i+1]))` and goes long when that is > 0. For any
+price above 1 this is always positive, so the signal is a **static long
+(buy-and-hold)**, not a momentum rule: the lookback is inert. Measured on the
+full series, 9/10 assets have exactly 1 weight change over 4,465 bars
+(frac_long 0.9986-0.9993) and NVDA has 2 (frac_long 0.5841, an early sub-$1
+split-adjusted period). For AAPL/AMZN/TSLA the repo momentum medians are exactly
+identical to a fresh always-long (all +1) walk-forward. A textbook
+trailing-return momentum (sign of `log(c_t) - log(c_{t-lb})`) would trade ~500-
+1200 times (AAPL 799 sign changes); the repo formula trades once. The earlier
+"identical medians across lookbacks" that was read as horizon-stable magnitude is
+the signature of this inert lookback, not evidence of robustness.
+
+Consequences for the cost question (reference lookback 5):
+- All 10 assets classify COST_ROBUST by the predeclared absolute-net rule
+  (net cost-inclusive medians essentially unchanged from zero to 2x costs:
+  AAPL 0.0700/0.0700/0.0700, AMZN 0.0760/0.0760/0.0760, NVDA
+  0.0130/0.0120/0.0110, etc.), but the informative subset (signals that actually
+  trade, TRADING turnover > 3) is **empty**. The robustness is real but vacuous:
+  a position that never rebalances barely pays costs.
+- The matched-null gap *widens* with cost (e.g. AAPL +0.0587/+0.0642/+0.0702)
+  because the iid coin-flip null churns ~2,384-2,953 times per series versus 1-2
+  for momentum; cost stress penalises the null far more. The gap is therefore
+  confounded and is stored only as a secondary, clearly-labelled diagnostic — it
+  must not be read as the edge strengthening under costs.
